 ### [Adaptive Step Size Ray Marching](2312-adaptive-step-size-ray-marching.md)
-A GPU-based direct volume rendering technique that reduces computational cost by varying the ray marching step size based on local volume density and distance from the camera. Unlike fixed-step methods that force a trade-off between quality and efficiency, this method uses fine steps inside dense regions and coarse steps in voids, often combined with temporal accumulation and noise-based jittering to maintain real-time performance. It sets itself apart from siblings like Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment by explicitly integrating a noise-driven step size modulation and temporal reprojection for handling camera motion.
+A GPU-based direct volume rendering technique that reduces computational cost by dynamically varying the ray marching step size based on local density and distance from the camera, often combined with temporal accumulation and denoising. It solves the trade-off between rendering quality and performance inherent in fixed-step linear sampling by using fine steps in high-density regions (or near the camera) and coarse steps in empty space or at a distance. Unlike its sibling [Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment](2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md), which may rely on static heuristics or gradient estimation, this method explicitly implements a noise-driven step size variation and temporal reprojection to maintain real-time performance on 3D and 4D volumes.
 
 ### [Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment](2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md)
-A heuristic optimization for distance-field ray marching that accelerates traversal through empty space by exponentially increasing the step size when consecutive samples indicate zero density. It solves the problem of wasted computation in large voids by skipping multiple voxels in a single step, setting itself apart from gradient-based methods by relying solely on occupancy status rather than field magnitude.
+A heuristic optimization for distance-field ray marching that accelerates traversal through empty or low-density regions by exponentially increasing the step size. It solves the problem of redundant sampling in sparse volumes, such as atmospheric clouds or distant geometry, where uniform steps waste computational resources. It sets itself apart from gradient-driven methods by relying on occupancy/density checks rather than field derivatives, and from fixed adaptive schemes by using a multiplicative doubling strategy that reacts to consecutive empty samples.
+
+### [Variance-based Step Size Adaptation](13161-variance-based-step-size-adaptation.md)
+A preprocessing technique that determines the adaptive ray traversal step size ($\Delta t$) for volume rendering by calculating the statistical variance of voxel data within spatial partitions called "bricks". It solves the problem of selecting an appropriate step size for sparse or local ray traversal without the computational expense of calculating iso-surface normals at runtime. By mapping brick variance to a scaling coefficient, it balances rendering speed and image quality, allowing larger steps in homogeneous regions and smaller steps in high-frequency areas.
 
